\description{
\code{draw()} sets the sample size or sampling fraction, the selection method,
and whatever that method needs: a measure of size for PPS, auxiliary
variables for balanced sampling, coordinates for spatial spread. Every
stage in a sampling design ends with \code{draw()}, which closes it. A second
\code{draw()}, or a \code{\link[=stratify_by]{stratify_by()}} or \code{\link[=cluster_by]{cluster_by()}} after it, is refused, and
\code{\link[=sampling_design]{sampling_design()}} describes the stage grammar.
}
\details{
\subsection{Sizes from svyplan}{

A svyplan size or plan object is read through svyplan's documented
coercions, stage by stage for cluster and stratified multistage plans.
An allocation from \code{\link[svyplan:n_alloc]{svyplan::n_alloc()}} or \code{\link[svyplan:n_alloc_joint]{svyplan::n_alloc_joint()}}
that plans PSUs gives stage 1 its whole PSU counts (\code{n_1_int}) and each
later stage k its whole take per parent unit (\code{take_k_int}), up to the
plan's \code{stages}. A plan giving one size for a stage stratified without
\code{alloc} is refused (\code{samplyr_error_svyplan_total_per_stratum}) rather
than taken in every stratum. A plan for a stage stratified by several
variables is matched on those columns of its table, which svyplan
carries from the frame's character and factor columns, and a plan
without them is refused (\code{samplyr_error_svyplan_multivariable_strata}).
A precision assessment from a \verb{prec_*()} function is not a size and is
refused. So is an \code{\link[svyplan:n_panel]{svyplan::n_panel()}} plan, which sizes a whole panel,
and the refusal names the count to pass instead. A fixed panel issues
\code{ceiling(plan$n_issued)} units. A rotating panel is issued selection by
selection, and how many units its startup takes depends on its \code{start}
and \code{rotation}, which \code{\link[svyplan:design_schedule]{svyplan::design_schedule()}} resolves. Each
selection's \code{operational_issue} in the schedule's \code{selections} table is
the count to draw, a count of PSUs when the schedule was built with
\code{take}, and with subrounds the sum of each panel's rounded issue.
\code{\link[=rotation_program]{rotation_program()}} checks the count against the schedule.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{issue <- with(schedule$selections, setNames(operational_issue, selection))
startup <- sampling_design() |> draw(n = issue[["startup"]])
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The planning choices are covered in
\code{vignette("joint-allocation", package = "svyplan")}.

A certainty-aware \code{\link[svyplan:n_alloc_joint]{svyplan::n_alloc_joint()}} plan, solved with a \code{psu}
register carrying \code{psu_id}, is given at a clustered stage 1 stratified by
the plan's one stratum column, and again at stage 2 for the per-PSU
takes. The takes may also come lower, below stages that keep one unit of
each PSU, such as \code{draw(n = 1)} of a segment per EA, and are then taken
inside that unit. Stage 1 takes every certainty PSU and draws exactly the
plan's remainder per stratum. A plan solved with \code{n_psu_per_zone = 2}
draws two PSUs from each of its zones instead, records the zone in
\code{.zone_1}, and exports one variance stratum per zone. With
\code{n_psu_per_zone = 1} it draws one PSU per zone, records the plan's
variance group in \code{.variance_group_1}, and exports the groups as variance
strata (\link{variance_estimation}). Stage 1's method must be
\code{pps_systematic}, \code{pps_brewer}, \code{pps_cps} or \code{pps_sampford}, with \code{mos}
equal to the register's \code{N}. The frame's PSU ids are matched to \code{psu_id}
by value, so both must be numbers or both labels, and 64-bit integer ids
(bit64) are matched exactly. A design file holds such ids only from
-2^53 to 2^53 (\code{\link[=write_design]{write_design()}}). The take stage selects
by \code{srswor} or \code{systematic}, and stratifying there requires an \code{alloc}
method. Arguments the plan owns (\code{frac}, \code{certainty_size},
\code{certainty_prop}, \code{min_n}, \code{max_n}, and at stage 1 \code{alloc}) are refused
alongside it. \code{\link[svyplan:merge_psus]{svyplan::merge_psus()}} merges PSUs smaller than the take.
}

\subsection{Certainty selection}{

In PPS without-replacement sampling, very large units can have
theoretical inclusion probabilities above 1. Certainty selection takes
them with probability 1 before sampling the remainder, and marks them in
the \code{.certainty_k} column described in \link{sample_columns}. It is available
for the WOR PPS methods (\code{pps_systematic}, \code{pps_brewer}, \code{pps_cps},
\code{pps_sampford}, \code{pps_poisson}, \code{pps_sps}, \code{pps_pareto}), for the methods
that can take a unit more than once (\code{pps_multinomial}, \code{pps_chromy},
\code{pps_systematic_mr}), and for methods registered with \code{type = "wor"} or
\code{"wr"}. A pool censused under
\code{certainty_overflow = "allow"} has stage weight 1, but in a multistage
design the final \code{.weight} can still exceed 1 because it compounds all
stage weights.

Without a declared rule, the fixed-size methods spread \code{n} by
\code{sondage::inclusion_prob()}. A unit whose share of its pool's size is
above \code{1 / n} gets chance 1, and the rest of the take is spread over the
other units, repeatedly until no chance exceeds 1. Such units are
selected with certainty, marked in \code{.certainty_k}, and counted in \code{n}.
\code{\link[=execute]{execute()}} reports them with \code{samplyr_message_certainty_selected}.
\code{certainty_size} and \code{certainty_prop} state a rule in the design instead,
which also lets the threshold sit below 1. A unit drawn with chance 0.98
is in practice a certainty unit with an unstable weight.

A method that can take a unit more than once needs no rule, since a large
unit gets several hits, each with a subsample of its own at the next
stage. Under \code{certainty_size} or \code{certainty_prop} such a unit is taken
once instead, with weight 1, and the remaining \code{n - n_c} hits are drawn by
the method over the remaining sizes, where a unit can still be hit more
than once. A certainty PSU then has one subsample at the next stage. When
the size measure counts the units below, a take of \code{m} per hit is
self-weighting, and a certainty PSU keeps the same weight if its take is
\code{m} times its size over the remainder's interval, the remaining size
total divided by \code{n - n_c}. The export treats the certainty units as a
take-all stratum and the remaining hits as drawn with replacement.
\code{pps_systematic_mr} with \code{certainty_size} corresponds to SAS SURVEYSELECT
\code{METHOD=PPS_SYS} with \verb{CERTSIZE=}, and \code{pps_multinomial} to
\code{METHOD=PPS_WR}. SAS has no \verb{CERTSIZE=P=} for these methods, and no
certainty option for its Chromy method.

With \code{pps_poisson}, both \code{n} and \code{frac} give a target expected total
that includes certainty units, \code{frac} as the unrounded \code{frac * N} over
the original pool. After \code{n_cert} certainty units are
selected, the remaining probabilities are
\code{pmin(1, (target - n_cert) * mos / sum(mos))} over the remaining units'
sizes. Thus \code{n = 5} and \code{frac = 0.5} on ten units give the same
probabilities, with or without a certainty rule. Clipping at one does not
redistribute the excess to other units, so the expected size can fall
below the target. \code{\link[=frame_summary]{frame_summary()}} reports both \code{n_target} and
\code{n_expected}.
}

\subsection{Control sorting}{

Control sorting orders the frame before selection, giving implicit
stratification with the systematic and sequential methods (\code{systematic},
\code{pps_systematic}, \code{pps_systematic_mr}, \code{pps_chromy}), where the sample
spreads evenly across the sorted variables. Other methods accept \code{control} but get no implicit
stratification from it. The sort can change which units a given seed
draws, but the sample does not spread over the sorted variables. A
coordinated \code{pps_sps} draw with \code{prn}, for example, keeps nothing of a
sort by district. "Spreading the sample" in \link{selection_methods} compares
the sort with the other ways to spread a sample.

Nested sorting (the default, \code{control = c(var1, var2, var3)}) sorts
ascending by each variable in turn. Serpentine sorting
(\code{control = serp(var1, var2, var3)}) alternates direction at each
hierarchy level, which minimizes the jumps between adjacent units. For a
geographic hierarchy, the last district of region 1 is then adjacent to
the last district of region 2.

With \code{\link[=stratify_by]{stratify_by()}}, sorting is applied within each stratum, combining
explicit stratification for variance control with implicit
stratification for sample spread.
}
}
\examples{
# Simple random sample of 100 EAs
sampling_design() |>
  draw(n = 100) |>
  execute(bfa_eas, seed = 1)

# Systematic sample of 10\%
sampling_design() |>
  draw(frac = 0.10, method = "systematic") |>
  execute(bfa_eas, seed = 123)

# PPS sample of EAs using household count
sampling_design() |>
  cluster_by(ea_id) |>
  draw(n = 50, method = "pps_brewer", mos = households) |>
  execute(bfa_eas, seed = 42)

# Systematic PPS on a frame sorted by province and commune, as many
# household survey protocols ask, which spreads the EAs over both
sampling_design() |>
  stratify_by(region, urban_rural) |>
  cluster_by(ea_id) |>
  draw(n = 2, method = "pps_systematic", mos = households,
       control = c(province, commune)) |>
  execute(bfa_eas, seed = 7)

# Bernoulli sampling with frac (random sample size, expected ~5\%)
sampling_design() |>
  draw(frac = 0.05, method = "bernoulli") |>
  execute(ken_enterprises, seed = 12345)

# Bernoulli sampling with expected n (converted to frac = 500/N)
sampling_design() |>
  draw(n = 500, method = "bernoulli") |>
  execute(bfa_eas, seed = 42)

# Stratified with different sizes per stratum (data frame)
region_sizes <- data.frame(
  region = levels(bfa_eas$region),
  n = c(20, 12, 25, 18, 22, 16, 14, 15, 20, 18, 12, 10, 8)
)
sampling_design() |>
  stratify_by(region) |>
  draw(n = region_sizes) |>
  execute(bfa_eas, seed = 123)

# Stratified with different rates per stratum (named vector)
sampling_design() |>
  stratify_by(size_class) |>
  draw(frac = c(Small = 0.02, Medium = 0.10, Large = 0.50)) |>
  execute(ken_enterprises, seed = 42)

# Neyman allocation with minimum 2 per stratum (for variance estimation)
sampling_design() |>
  stratify_by(region, alloc = "neyman", variance = bfa_eas_variance) |>
  draw(n = 150, min_n = 2) |>
  execute(bfa_eas, seed = 2026)

# Proportional allocation with min and max bounds
sampling_design() |>
  stratify_by(region, alloc = "proportional") |>
  draw(n = 200, min_n = 10, max_n = 50) |>
  execute(bfa_eas, seed = 1)

# 0.5\% of the EAs in each province, at least 3 and at most 8 (SPSS rule)
sampling_design() |>
  stratify_by(province) |>
  draw(frac = 0.005, min_n = 3, max_n = 8, round = "nearest") |>
  execute(bfa_eas, seed = 4)

# Chromy's sequential PPS (SAS PPS_SEQ) with serpentine ordering, the
# order SAS gives CONTROL variables by default
sampling_design() |>
  draw(n = 100, method = "pps_chromy", mos = households,
       control = serp(region, province)) |>
  execute(bfa_eas, seed = 2)

# Systematic PPS with minimum replacement (SAS PPS_SYS). The largest EA
# of Poura holds more than an eighth of its households, so it expects
# 1.74 of 8 hits and is drawn once or twice, here twice. Each hit is a
# row, numbered by `.draw_1`, with weight 1 / 1.74.
poura <- subset(bfa_eas, commune == "Poura")
poura_sample <- sampling_design() |>
  draw(n = 8, method = "pps_systematic_mr", mos = households) |>
  execute(poura, seed = 1)
as.data.frame(poura_sample)[c("ea_id", "households", ".draw_1", ".weight")]

# The export reads the hits as drawn with replacement, and warns that the
# frame order can make that variance far too small or too large
if (requireNamespace("survey", quietly = TRUE)) {
  poura_svy <- as_svydesign(poura_sample)
}

# Control sorting with nested (standard) ordering
sampling_design() |>
  draw(n = 100, method = "systematic",
       control = c(region, province)) |>
  execute(bfa_eas, seed = 3)

# Combined explicit stratification with control sorting within strata
sampling_design() |>
  stratify_by(urban_rural) |>
  draw(n = 50, method = "systematic",
       control = serp(region, province)) |>
  execute(bfa_eas, seed = 25)

# PPS with certainty selection (absolute threshold)
# Large EAs selected with certainty, rest sampled with PPS
sampling_design() |>
  stratify_by(region) |>
  draw(n = 100, method = "pps_brewer", mos = households,
       certainty_size = 800) |>
  execute(bfa_eas, seed = 3)

# PPS with certainty selection (proportional threshold)
# EAs with >= 10\% of stratum total selected with certainty
sampling_design() |>
  stratify_by(region) |>
  draw(n = 100, method = "pps_systematic", mos = households,
       certainty_prop = 0.10) |>
  execute(bfa_eas, seed = 321)

# Stratum-specific certainty thresholds (data frame)
cert_thresholds <- data.frame(
  region = levels(bfa_eas$region),
  certainty_size = c(700, 450, 800, 850, 750, 800, 550,
                     450, 700, 950, 750, 600, 480)
)
sampling_design() |>
  stratify_by(region) |>
  draw(n = 100, method = "pps_brewer", mos = households,
       certainty_size = cert_thresholds) |>
  execute(bfa_eas, seed = 424)

# A two-stage svyplan allocation gives each stage its whole sizes: EAs
# per stratum at stage 1, households per EA at stage 2. Check that the
# search found a whole design before fieldwork.
sizes <- aggregate(cbind(N = households, N_1 = 1) ~ urban_rural,
                   bfa_eas, sum)
plan_frame <- data.frame(
  stratum = sizes$urban_rural, N = sizes$N, N_1 = sizes$N_1,
  sd = c(0.48, 0.45), mean = c(0.35, 0.28),
  icc_1 = c(0.08, 0.04), cost_1 = c(600, 300), cost_2 = c(60, 40)
)
plan <- svyplan::n_alloc(plan_frame, cv = 0.03)
plan$operational$status
plan$detail[c("stratum", "n_1_int", "take_2_int")]
two_stage <- sampling_design() |>
  add_stage(label = "EA") |>
  stratify_by(urban_rural) |>
  cluster_by(ea_id) |>
  draw(n = plan, method = "pps_brewer", mos = households) |>
  add_stage(label = "Household") |>
  stratify_by(urban_rural) |>
  draw(n = plan)
selected_eas <- execute(two_stage, bfa_eas, stages = 1, seed = 3)
table(selected_eas$urban_rural)

# A plan fitted on a register of EAs takes the EAs it holds certain, draws
# the rest, and takes each EA's own number of households
eas <- bfa_eas[
  bfa_eas$province == "Oubritenga" & bfa_eas$households >= 12,
]
register <- data.frame(
  psu_id = eas$ea_id, stratum = as.character(eas$urban_rural),
  N = eas$households
)
register_frame <- aggregate(N ~ stratum, register, sum)
register_frame$take_2 <- 12
fit <- svyplan::n_alloc_joint(
  register_frame,
  data.frame(stratum = register_frame$stratum, name = "coverage",
             p = 0.4, icc_1 = 0.05),
  targets = data.frame(name = "coverage", cv = 0.04),
  psu = register
)
fit$operational[c("n", "all_pass", "status")]
fit$detail[c("stratum", "n_psu_certain", "n_psu_draw")]
sampling_design() |>
  add_stage(label = "EA") |>
  stratify_by(urban_rural) |>
  cluster_by(ea_id) |>
  draw(n = fit, method = "pps_brewer", mos = households) |>
  add_stage(label = "Household") |>
  draw(n = fit)

}
